# Summary of Findings for Relevant Literatures

1. ‘A review on the generation, determination and mitigation of Urban Heat Island’: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1001074208600194
  This paper provides a comprehensive analysis of the factors influencing UHI:
    A. Primary Heat Sources:
      1. Anthropogenic Heat: vehicles, power plants, air conditioners, and industrial activities, can be estimated based on energy consumption and population density
      2. Indirect Solar Heating (ISH): heat stored and re-radiated by urban structures. Dependent on building materials, urban density, and albedo, and affected by sky view factor(i.e., the openness of urban canyons to the sky)
    B. Environmental and Meteorological Factors:
      1. Wind Speed & Cloud Cover: UHI intensity is negatively correlated with wind speed and cloud cover
      2. Solar Radiation: Higher daytime solar radiation increases UHI, but nighttime cooling is also affected
      3. Humidity & Latent Heat Flux: Evapotranspiration plays a role in cooling; areas with more vegetation exhibit lower UHI intensity
    C. Urban Design and Land Cover:
      1. Building Material & Thermal Properties: Low-albedo materials (e.g., asphalt, concrete) retain more heat; High thermal admittance materials store and slowly release heat
      2. Green Spaces & Vegetation Cover: greenery reduce UHI through shading and evapotranspiration; reduced vegetation leads to lower latent heat flux and higher surface temperatures
      3. Urban Geometry & Sky View Factor (SVF): Tall, dense buildings trap heat and reduce nighttime cooling; SVF influences long-wave radiation emission and affects heat dissipation
      4. Population Density & Urban Activity: Higher population densities correlate with increased anthropogenic heat emissions; However, the relationship is complex and depends on urban planning

2. ‘A statistical model for the urban heat island and its application to a climate change scenario’: https://rmets.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/joc.2348
  It presents a linear statistical model to predict the nocturnal UHI intensity, based on meteorological observations:
    A. Key Factors for UHI Prediction:
      1. Wind Speed (FF): Negative correlation: Higher wind speeds reduce UHI by increasing ventilation and heat dispersion
      2. Previous Day’s Cloud Cover (CC): Negative correlation: Clouds absorb and emit long-wave radiation, reducing temperature variations and heat storage in urban surfaces
      3. Relative Humidity (RH): Negative correlation: Higher humidity increases condensation in rural areas before urban areas, reducing the temperature difference
      4. Air Pressure (P): Weak influence: Explains only 7.6% of UHI variance
      5. Water Vapor Pressure (WVP): No significant relationship with UHI (R² = 0.2%)
    B. Statistical Model for UHI Prediction:
      ΔTu−r = aFF + bCC + cRH + d + ϵ
      This model explains 42% of UHI variance

3. 'ArcUHI: A GIS add-in for automated modelling of the Urban Heat Island effect through machine learning': https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2212095522001213
  A. This paper confirms some of the factors discovered in the first paper:
    1. Building Height: Taller buildings intensified the street canyon effect, trapping heat
    2. Albedo: Higher albedo values (reflective surfaces) reduced UHI intensity
    3. Sky View Factor (SVF): Low SVF (more obstructed sky) was associated with higher UHI
    4. Elevation and Slope: Higher elevations exhibited lower UHI due to heat dissipation
    5. Urban Fabric and Roads: Increased built-up areas correlated with higher UHI intensity
  B. It tested 3 models:
    1. Multiple Linear Regression (MLR): Provides interpretability but has low predictive accuracy
    2. Support Vector Regression (SVR): Captures nonlinear relationships but requires significant computational resources
    3. Random Forest Regression (RFR): Achieved the best performance with high predictive accuracy

4. 'Development of a model for urban heat island prediction using neural network techniques': https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S221067071100014X
  This paper uesed artificial neural networks:
    A. Data:
      1. Date & Time: Converted into numerical values (days of the year, minutes of the day)
      2. Ambient Temperature (°C): Measured at different locations
      3. Global Solar Radiation (W/m²): Collected from the National Observatory of Athens
      4. Data were normalized in the range [-1,1] to improve ANN training
    B. 3 Neural Networks Tested:
      1. Cascade Neural Network (CNN)
      2. Elman Neural Network (ENN)
      3. Feed-Forward Neural Network (FNN)
    C. Training Algorithms Tested:
      1. Levenberg-Marquardt (trainlm)
      2. Scaled Conjugate Gradient (trainscg)
      3. BFGS Quasi-Newton (trainbfg)
      4. Gradient Descent (traingd, traingdm)
      5. Resilient Backpropagation (traingnrp)
    D. Network Configuration:
      1. Hidden Layers: 1-3 layers, each with 20–40 neurons
      2. Activation Function: Tangent sigmoid function
    E. Best Performing Neural Network:
      1. The Elman Neural Network (ENN) with the Levenberg-Marquardt training function achieved the best performance.
      2. Evaluated using: MSE, Prediction accuracy, R2
